@spanishpear Thanks for bringing this up - this is definitely something we need to address at some point. That said, we're currently swamped with other priorities through November, so we won’t be able to give this the attention it deserves until then.

Even if you're able to take on the work of updating this repo (which we’d really appreciate!), it would require a major version bump. That, in turn, means we’d need to update all of the oclif and Salesforce plugins - about 50 in total - to use the new major version. While the work isn’t massive, it does require coordination and time we just don’t have right now.

Also, since this would be a major release, we’d want to take the opportunity to evaluate any other potential breaking changes - which we unfortunately don’t have the capacity to do at the moment either.

> > There is an easier, more elegant solution. You can implement a hook, and load the dotenv in the hook. You can even parse the command args for a custom `--env` path and load that custom path using `dotenv.config({ path });`. Using the `init` hook will ensure it's there for the entire execution.
> 
> Interested to see how that looks like, [@visargashi](https://github.com/visargashi). Would you be able to provide a reference on how that could be implemented?

Create a new hook, e.g. `npx oclif@latest generate hook dotenv-init`, then in the generated file, you add dotenv, e.g.:

```typescript
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
import { Hook } from '@oclif/core';

const hook: Hook<'init'> = async function (opts) {
  // you can specify additional location here, using `opts`
  dotenv.config();
};

export default hook;
```

@AllanOricil I like the idea of accepting a function but I don't think this would solve my issue.

I have many optional flags but one of three flags must be provided to make the command useful, but this function seems like it'll set each one of these flags to be required as far as I understand the idea.

My issue maybe could be solved by using a special property in the flags object:

```js
static flags = {
  _required: {
    flags: ['flag-a', 'flag-b', 'flag-c'],
    type: 'one' // or 'all' or 'some' or 'any'
  }
}
```
